WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.882
This final module brings the course back to the main idea: application security is part of a practical defensive security program.

2
00:00:07.882 --> 00:00:19.077
The OWASP Top 10 gives teams a clear language for common application risks, while the CIS Controls help translate those risks into safeguards, ownership, and repeatable work.

3
00:00:19.077 --> 00:00:23.358
A weakness in an application rarely stays inside the application team.

4
00:00:23.358 --> 00:00:33.685
It can affect identity, data protection, secure configuration, vulnerability management, logging, monitoring, incident response, and customer trust.

5
00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:38.783
That is why secure development needs to connect with operations and security processes.

6
00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:41.527
Use the mappings in this course as a planning aid.

7
00:00:41.527 --> 00:00:51.704
For each application risk, ask which CIS Controls safeguards help prevent the issue, which safeguards help detect abuse, and which safeguards support response and recovery.

8
00:00:51.704 --> 00:00:55.466
Then confirm who owns the work and what evidence shows that it is happening.

9
00:00:55.466 --> 00:00:57.821
Good evidence does not need to be theatrical.

10
00:00:57.821 --> 00:01:11.111
Tickets, scan results, code review records, change approvals, architecture notes, test results, access reviews, and monitoring records can all help show that security work is repeatable and improving.

11
00:01:11.111 --> 00:01:16.399
After this course, a practical next step is to review a real application or service.

12
00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:26.457
Pick one OWASP risk, identify the relevant CIS Controls safeguards, confirm current controls and gaps, assign owners, and document the next improvement.

13
00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:32.224
Small, consistent improvements are how application security becomes durable defensive practice.
